<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stainmore</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stainmore Pass</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stainmore is a high pass across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> between the Eden valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a> and Teesdale, followed by a Roman road and the modern A66. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it marked a frontier of the Northumbrian and Cumbrian lands, and the stump of the Rey Cross stands on the summit. According to later tradition, the last Norse king of York, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-bloodaxe">Eric Bloodaxe</a>, was killed there in 954.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stainmore.jpg" alt="Stainmore" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ANDREW SMITH · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mousegill_with_Great_Knipe_in_the_background_-_geograph.org.uk_-_598472.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
